  "notes": [
    "Standard Gibbs energies of formation at 298.15 K, 1 bar standard state, kJ/mol.",
    "Phases matter: H2O(l) is -237.13 and H2O(g) is -228.57, an 8.6 kJ/mol swing that decides whether methane combustion reads as -818 or -801 kJ/mol. Every rule therefore states a phase.",
    "Aqueous values are for the 1 M standard state of the dissolved species. An aqueous salt is the sum of its ionic values on the conventional scale (H+(aq) = 0 by definition), which is the convention tests/thermo-table.test.ts re-derives to catch transcription errors; the individual ions are listed as their own entries so that sum is checkable.",
    "A simple substance in its standard reference state is 0 by definition, not by measurement. Those entries say so in 'source'.",
    "Single ions are written with a charge suffix (Na+, Ca2+, SO4^2-); the loader canonicalises that suffix, so 'SO4^2-' and 'SO4-2' reach the same entry.",
    "NH4Cl(s) is the one entry where the tabulated Gibbs energy and its own tabulated enthalpy/entropy disagree by more than a kilojoule; the tabulated value is kept and the discrepancy noted on the entry.",
    "Scope is deliberate: a species is here only if its value is tabulated in a standard reference or cross-checkable against one (a solubility product, a Hess cycle). A species the rules can propose but that is not listed is reported as unknown, never guessed."
  ],
